<div class="tab-panel" id="panel-faq">
<div class="faq-wrap">

  <div class="faq-hero">
    <h1>Docs &amp; FAQ</h1>
    <p>How to use CircaThread, definitions for your methods section, and answers to common questions. All processing is performed locally in your browser — no data is uploaded to any server.</p>
  </div>

  <!-- ── GETTING STARTED ─────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="faq-section">
    <div class="faq-section-title">Getting started</div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">What file format does CircaThread expect? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>CircaThread reads <strong>comma-separated values (.csv)</strong> or plain-text files exported directly from a LumiCycle instrument or compatible luminometry system. Each file should represent a single biological replicate (one well, one dish, or one animal).</p>
        <p>The expected structure is two columns: <strong>elapsed time</strong> (in hours) in the first column and <strong>raw luminescence counts</strong> (counts per second or RLU) in the second. A single header row is detected and skipped automatically. Files with additional columns are accepted — only the first two data columns are used.</p>
        <p>Example:</p>
        <code style="display:block;white-space:pre;font-size:.8rem;background:var(--bg4);padding:.6rem .8rem;border-radius:2px;margin:.6rem 0;overflow-x:auto">Time (h), Counts (CPS)
0.00, 184302
0.10, 183912
0.20, 181450
...</code>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Can I load data from multiple replicates at once? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>Yes. All three modules support multi-file upload — use <strong>Browse or drop CSV files</strong> and select multiple files simultaneously, or drop them all at once. Each file is treated as one independent replicate.</p>
        <p>Files loaded in any tab are automatically offered to the other tabs via a <strong>shared file banner</strong> that appears at the top of the drop zone when you switch tabs, so you only need to upload once per session.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Is my data uploaded to the internet? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p><strong>No.</strong> CircaThread runs entirely in your browser. All file reading, peak detection, and calculations happen locally in JavaScript. No data is transmitted to any server, and the tool works fully offline once the page has loaded.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── CircATreat ────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="faq-section">
    <div class="faq-section-title">CircATreat — Treatment Scheduler <span class="faq-tag treat">CircATreat</span></div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">What does CircATreat calculate? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>CircATreat converts a desired <strong>circadian time (CT)</strong> or phase-relative description (Peak, Trough, h+6, etc.) into a <strong>real-world clock time</strong> at which to add a compound to your cells or animals.</p>
        <p>It does this by detecting the most recent peak in your luminescence trace, anchoring that peak to the CT of your reporter cassette, and projecting forward or backward to the target time within the current or next cycle.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">What is a reporter cassette and why does it matter? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>A <strong>reporter cassette</strong> is the gene whose promoter drives luciferase expression in your cell line (e.g. <em>Bmal1</em>::Luc, <em>Per2</em>::Luc). Each reporter reaches its transcriptional peak at a different circadian time:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Bmal1::Luc</strong> — peak at CT0 (subjective dawn)</li>
          <li><strong>Per2::Luc</strong> — peak at CT12 (subjective dusk)</li>
          <li><strong>Cry1::Luc</strong> — peak at CT16</li>
          <li><strong>Rev-erbα::Luc</strong> — peak at CT8</li>
          <li><strong>Dbp::Luc</strong> — peak at CT8</li>
        </ul>
        <p>If you request a treatment at <strong>CT8</strong> using a <em>Bmal1</em>::Luc reporter, the calculation shifts by 8 h ahead of the detected luminescence peak. The same CT8 request with a <em>Per2</em>::Luc reporter shifts 8 h ahead of a peak that is already 12 h later in the cycle — producing a different real-world clock time. Selecting the wrong cassette will silently shift all your treatment times by the phase offset between reporters.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">How do I specify treatment times? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>Enter one or more times in the <strong>Treatment times</strong> field, separated by commas. The following formats are accepted:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>CT0</code>, <code>ZT12</code>, <code>CT20</code> — a specific circadian or Zeitgeber time (reporter-cassette aware)</li>
          <li><code>Peak</code> — at the peak of the luminescence cycle (≡ the reporter's peak CT)</li>
          <li><code>Trough</code> — at the trough (half-period after peak)</li>
          <li><code>h+6</code>, <code>h-3</code> — hours after or before the detected peak</li>
          <li><code>6</code> — bare number, treated as hours after peak</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Note:</strong> CT/ZT entries are only meaningful when a reporter cassette is selected. If no cassette is chosen, they are treated as hours-after-peak and a warning banner is shown.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">What is the difference between Absolute and Optimum results? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p><strong>Absolute</strong> results show each replicate's predicted treatment time independently. This is useful when replicates are on clearly different cycle offsets (e.g. different passage numbers or recording start times) and you intend to treat each dish at its own optimal time.</p>
        <p><strong>Optimum</strong> shows a consensus clock time across all replicates — the single time at which all replicates are within ±20 minutes of their target phase. A yellow warning is displayed if agreement within that window cannot be achieved, indicating that your replicates are too desynchronised for a single-timepoint treatment.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Methods section wording for CircATreat <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <div class="methods-box">
          <strong>Suggested methods text:</strong><br>
          Circadian treatment times were determined using CircATreat (CircaThread v1; Topping et al., 2026). Briefly, raw luminescence traces from [reporter] reporter cells were analysed to detect the most recent peak. The circadian phase of that peak was anchored to CT[X] based on the known peak circadian time of the [reporter]::Luc cassette. The desired treatment circadian time (CT[Y]) was then converted to a real-world clock time by projecting [forward/backward] within the estimated free-running period of [~N] h. Where multiple replicates were used, a consensus treatment time was selected at which all replicates fell within ±20 min of the target phase (Optimum mode).
        </div>
        <p>Replace bracketed terms with your specific reporter, CTs, and period estimate.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── CircATrace ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="faq-section">
    <div class="faq-section-title">CircATrace — Publication Plotter <span class="faq-tag trace">CircATrace</span></div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">What does CircATrace plot? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>CircATrace overlays multiple luminescence traces on a shared time axis as a publication-ready figure. It supports:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Raw or baseline-normalised (first-value) traces</li>
          <li>Mean ± SEM or ± SD error bands across replicates grouped by colour</li>
          <li>Light / dark cycle shading with configurable LD period</li>
          <li>Custom axis labels, title, and time range</li>
          <li>SVG and PNG export at screen resolution</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Methods section wording for CircATrace <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <div class="methods-box">
          <strong>Suggested methods text:</strong><br>
          Luminescence traces were plotted using CircATrace (CircaThread v1; Topping et al., 2026). Raw counts-per-second data were [normalised to the first recorded value / plotted without normalisation]. Where multiple biological replicates were included, data are presented as mean ± SEM [or SD]. Shaded regions indicate [12:12 h / N:N h] light:dark cycles aligned to the recording start time.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── CircATrends ────────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="faq-section">
    <div class="faq-section-title">CircATrends — Statistical Analysis <span class="faq-tag trend">CircATrends</span></div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">How does CircATrends detect peaks and troughs? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>CircATrends applies a <strong>smoothing pass</strong> (moving average) to reduce noise, then detects local maxima and minima by identifying points at which the gradient changes sign from positive to negative (peaks) or negative to positive (troughs). Peaks and troughs are constrained to be separated by at least a minimum inter-event interval (half the estimated period) to avoid detecting noise as independent events.</p>
        <p>A <strong>baseline correction</strong> (linear detrending) is applied before peak detection to account for the damping of bioluminescence signal over extended recordings.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">What parameters does CircATrends calculate? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Period (h)</strong> — mean time between consecutive peaks</li>
          <li><strong>Phase / acrophase (h)</strong> — time of the first detected peak relative to the recording start or a user-defined reference point</li>
          <li><strong>Amplitude (counts/sec)</strong> — half the peak-to-trough difference, representing the oscillation half-range</li>
          <li><strong>Magnitude (counts/sec)</strong> — full peak-to-trough difference</li>
          <li><strong>Rising period (h)</strong> — mean time from trough to the next peak</li>
          <li><strong>Falling period (h)</strong> — mean time from peak to the next trough</li>
        </ul>
        <p>These parameters are calculated per sample and exported as an .xlsx file. Peak and trough XY coordinates are also exported for downstream analysis in R or GraphPad Prism.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">What is amplitude vs. magnitude? <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p><strong>Amplitude</strong> in CircaTrends follows the convention used in cosinor analysis: it is the <em>half-range</em> of the oscillation, equivalent to the distance from the MESOR (midline estimating statistic of rhythm) to the peak. Numerically, this is half the peak-to-trough difference.</p>
        <p><strong>Magnitude</strong> is the full peak-to-trough difference (sometimes called peak-to-trough amplitude in the literature). This is often the more intuitive measure and is commonly reported for bioluminescence data.</p>
        <p>Both values are provided in the exported table. We recommend specifying which measure you report in your methods section.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Methods section wording for CircATrends <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <div class="methods-box">
          <strong>Suggested methods text:</strong><br>
          Circadian parameters were extracted using CircATrends (CircaThread v1; Topping et al., 2026). Raw luminescence data were linearly detrended to remove baseline drift, then smoothed with a moving-average filter prior to peak and trough detection. Local maxima and minima were identified as sign changes in the first derivative, constrained to a minimum inter-event interval of [N] h. Period was calculated as the mean inter-peak interval; amplitude as the half peak-to-trough range; and acrophase as the time of the first detected peak relative to [recording start / ZT0]. Parameters were exported as .xlsx and statistical comparisons were performed in [GraphPad Prism / R].
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ── DEFINITIONS ───────────────────────────────── -->
  <div class="faq-section">
    <div class="faq-section-title">Key definitions</div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Circadian Time (CT) vs Zeitgeber Time (ZT) <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p><strong>Zeitgeber Time (ZT)</strong> describes time relative to an external entraining signal (typically lights-on = ZT0, lights-off = ZT12 in a 12:12 LD cycle). It is an environmental reference.</p>
        <p><strong>Circadian Time (CT)</strong> describes time relative to the animal or cell's intrinsic clock, measured under free-running conditions (constant darkness or constant light). CT0 is defined as the onset of subjective day. In a free-running system, CT and ZT may diverge as the endogenous period drifts from 24 h.</p>
        <p>In the context of CircATreat, CT and ZT are treated equivalently — both refer to a position on the ~24 h circadian cycle, anchored to your reporter cassette's known peak phase.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Period (τ, tau) <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>The <strong>period</strong> (τ, tau) is the time required to complete one full oscillation cycle. For a mammalian circadian clock in constant conditions, τ is typically ~24 h but varies between ~23–25 h depending on species, cell type, and genetic background. CircaTrends estimates τ as the mean interval between consecutive detected luminescence peaks.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Phase and acrophase <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p><strong>Phase</strong> refers to the position of a rhythm at a specified reference point. In circadian biology, phase is often reported as the time of peak (acrophase) relative to ZT0 or CT0.</p>
        <p><strong>Acrophase</strong> (from Greek <em>akros</em>, summit) is the time at which the rhythm reaches its maximum value within a cycle. In cosinor analysis, acrophase is expressed as a phase angle (degrees or radians); in CircaTrends it is expressed in hours relative to recording start or a user-defined reference.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Amplitude and MESOR <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p><strong>Amplitude</strong> is the half-range of the oscillation — the distance from the midline (MESOR) to the peak. A larger amplitude indicates a more robust, higher-contrast rhythm.</p>
        <p><strong>MESOR</strong> (Midline Estimating Statistic Of Rhythm) is the mean level of the fitted cosine curve, equivalent to the rhythm's vertical midpoint. In bioluminescence data, the MESOR reflects baseline luciferase expression independent of oscillation.</p>
        <p>CircaTrends approximates amplitude as half the peak-to-trough difference. For a pure cosine, this is exact; for asymmetric waveforms it is an approximation.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Entrainment and free-running period <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p><strong>Entrainment</strong> is the synchronisation of the circadian clock to an external zeitgeber (time-giver), typically the light-dark cycle. An entrained clock runs at exactly 24 h, phase-locked to the environment.</p>
        <p><strong>Free-running period</strong> is the intrinsic period of the clock in the absence of zeitgebers (constant conditions). In most mammalian cell lines and tissues, the free-running period is close to 24 h but rarely exactly 24 h. LumiCycle recordings are typically made under constant temperature and no imposed LD cycle, so the period you measure in CircATrends is the free-running period.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="faq-item">
      <button class="faq-q" onclick="faqToggle(this)">Bioluminescence reporter assay (LumiCycle) <span class="faq-chevron">▼</span></button>
      <div class="faq-a">
        <p>A <strong>bioluminescence reporter assay</strong> uses cells or tissue stably expressing a luciferase gene under the control of a circadian clock gene promoter (e.g. <em>Bmal1</em>::Luc, <em>Per2</em>::Luc). When the clock gene is transcriptionally active, luciferase is produced and emits light in the presence of its substrate (luciferin). Luminescence therefore oscillates with the circadian period, providing a real-time, non-invasive readout of clock gene expression.</p>
        <p>The <strong>LumiCycle</strong> (Actimetrics) is a photomultiplier-based device designed to record luminescence from cell culture dishes continuously over several days. Data are exported as time-stamped counts-per-second CSV files, which are the primary input for CircaThread.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

